{"question": "A 23-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 25 minutes after being involved in a high-speed motor vehicle collision in which he was the restrained driver. On questioning by the paramedics, he reported severe chest pain and mild dyspnea. On arrival, he is confused and unable to provide a history. His pulse is 93/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 91/65 mm Hg. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 88%. He is able to move his extremities in response to commands. He opens his eyes spontaneously. Pupils are equal and reactive to light. Examination shows multiple bruises over the trunk and extremities. There is a 3-cm (1.2-in) wound at the left fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line. There is jugular venous distention. Decreased breath sounds and hyperresonance on percussion are noted on the left. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "CT scan of the chest", "B": "Bronchoscopy", "C": "Emergency thoracotomy", "D": "Needle decompression", "E": "X-ray of the chest\n\""}, "pred_answer": "D", "gold_answer": "D", "question_domains": ["Emergency Medicine", "Trauma Surgery", "Pulmonology", "Cardiology", "Intensive Care Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Emergency Medicine", "Surgical Intervention"], "question_analyses": {"Emergency Medicine": "The patient described is a 23-year-old male who has been involved in a high-speed motor vehicle collision and is now presenting with severe chest pain, mild dyspnea, confusion, hypotension, tachypnea, and hypoxemia. The physical examination reveals multiple bruises, a wound at the left fifth intercostal space, jugular venous distention, decreased breath sounds, and hyperresonance on percussion on the left side of the chest. These findings suggest a significant traumatic injury, likely involving the chest. ### Interpretation of Condition:\n1. **Severe Chest Pain and Dyspnea**: These symptoms are indicative of a possible